<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anthonie Heinsius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1641">1641</a>–<a href="/years/1720">1720</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anthonie Heinsius was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-pensionary">Grand Pensionary</a> of Holland from 1689 until his death in 1720 and a close ally of the stadtholder and English king William III. After William's death in 1702 he directed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> policy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, working with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">Duke of Marlborough</a> and Prince Eugene against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The war's cost left the Republic heavily indebted, and the Peace of Utrecht of 1713 brought it few gains.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anthonie-heinsius.jpg" alt="Anthonie Heinsius" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerbrand van den Eeckhout · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1659_portrait_of_a_man,_Possibly_of_Anthonie_Heinsius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anthonie Heinsius was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a> on 23 November 1641 to Adriaan Heinsius, of one of the city's governing families, and Maria Dedel. He studied at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, took a doctorate in civil and canon law in 1662 and practised law in Delft until he became its pensionary in 1679. Delft then usually sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> against the foreign policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england">William III of Orange</a>, and Heinsius leaned the same way until a mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1682, where the French minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-michel-le-tellier-marquis-de-louvois">Louvois</a> threatened him with the Bastille, seems to have convinced him that the power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> was a danger.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Grand Pensionary Gaspar Fagel came to trust him, and after the deaths of Fagel and his interim successor Michiel ten Hove the States of Holland elected Heinsius on 26 May 1689, at William's insistence, and re-elected him every five years. He was among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> delegates who concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ryswick">Peace of Ryswick</a>, which let the Dutch garrison eight key cities of the Spanish Netherlands.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> he directed much of the Grand Alliance's political strategy. The States General bound Marlborough's command with twelve articles, and the duke relied on his good relations with Heinsius in dealing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> generals and field deputies. In the peace talks of 1709 and 1710 Heinsius insisted that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> use his own troops to drive his grandson from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the talks collapsed. When the British Tory ministry of Robert Harley negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on its own, the Dutch fought on until their defeat at Denain, and they signed a new Barrier Treaty on 30 January 1713.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The reproaches of 1712 shook his authority, but he stayed in office and, unmarried to the end, died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> in 1720 after more than 31 years as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-pensionary">Grand Pensionary</a>. The historian Darryl Dee called his modest office in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binnenhof">Binnenhof</a>, where he met Marlborough and Prince Eugene between campaigns, the true headquarters of the Grand Alliance. The French diplomat Torcy described him as polite, never harsh and without ostentation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anthonie Heinsius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.01,4.36&amp;year=1692" aria-label="Delft on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4648 366 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anthonie Heinsius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5098 636h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5098 636h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.01,4.36&amp;year=1692">What was here in 1692? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
